--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@
 
 ## Unreleased
 
+- `callers` of a Python method follows the method resolution order. `self.m()` and `cls.m()` reach the first class in the caller's C3 linearization that defines `m` (`class Child(Mixin, Base)` runs `Mixin.m`, not `Base.m`, and a subclass that overrides ends the walk), `super().m()` and `super(C, self).m()` reach the next definition after the calling class, and in a diamond (`D(B, C)` over `A`) the `super()` in `B` is also listed as a caller of `C.m`, marked `guess 1 of N`. A base from outside the project that stands before the definer marks the site a guess. Editing a class's bases re-scores those calls in the classes around it in other files. Schema 25; Python files re-parse once.
 - `callers` of a C function-pointer member lists the lines storing a function in it (`.irq_mask = foo_mask,`) as their own group after the call sites, `stores a function in it (not calls): N`, no longer among the references that are not calls, and they no longer make the answer read as incomplete. `callers` of a function counts the documentation lines naming it after the verdict (`plus N in docs (not code, not listed)`) and never lists them.
 - The SubagentStart brief no longer carries the swarm board protocol line by default: in A/B runs it gave parallel subagents nothing and cost more. `[hooks] swarm_board = true` in `config.toml` brings it back. The parent packet and parent read lines stay.
 
